  "content": {
    "title": "When is Bahama Breeze closing? What we know about Florida locations",
    "text": "Feb. 4, 2026, 11:13 a.m. ETDarden Restaurants announced it is shuttering all 28 of its remaining Bahama Breeze locations.Fourteen locations will be permanently closed by April 5, 2026, while the other 14 will be converted to other Darden brands.The company stated last year that the Bahama Breeze chain was no longer a strategic priority.Florida has the most Bahama Breeze locations, all of which will be either closed or converted.Bye-bye, Bahama Breeze! If you're a fan of the restaurant chain's coconut shrimp, jerk chicken pasta, chicken kabobs, or ribs with guava barbecue sauce, you only have a couple of months to get your last taste.Darden Restaurants Inc., which also operates Olive Garden, LongHorn Steakhouse, Yard House, The Capital Grille, The Capital Burger, Ruth’s Chris Steakhouse, Cheddar’s Scratch Kitchen, Chuy’s, Eddy V’s, and Seasons 52, announced on Tuesday, Feb. 3, that it's shuttering all of its 28 remaining Bahama Breeze locations.Here's when Bahama Breeze is expected to close its remaining locations, how many Bahama Breeze locations are in Florida, and where they are.When is Bahama Breeze closing?\"The 14 restaurants designated for permanent closure are expected to continue operating through April 5, 2026,\" the company said in a release.The other 14 locations are expected to be converted over the next 12-18 months, and are expected to stay open and operating until conversions begin.Why is Bahama Breeze closing?Last year, Darden Restaurants Inc. said that Bahama Breeze restaurants were \"no longer a strategic priority,\" and that it planned to investigate other options, such as selling or converting them to its other brands. Last May, Darden abruptly closed 15 Bahama Breeze locations across the U.S, including five in Florida.And this week, the Orlando-based company announced it would close half of the remaining locations and convert the rest.What will Bahama Breeze locations be converted to?As of Wednesday, Feb. 4, Darden has not yet specified what the 14 locations that aren't completely closing will be converted to.\"At this time, the company is not disclosing the specific Darden brands into which these locations will be converted,\" the company said on Feb. 3.Which Florida Bahama Breeze locations are closing?According to Darden's press release, the following locations in Florida will be completely closed by April 5, 2026:Jacksonville: 10205 Rivercoast DriveKissimmee: 1251 West Osceola Pkwy.Miami: 12395 S.W. 88th StreetPembroke Pines: 11000 Pines Blvd.Sanford: 1540 Rinehart RoadWhich Florida Bahama Breeze locations will be converted?Here are the remaining Florida Bahama Breeze locations that will be converted into other restaurants:Altamonte Springs: 499 E. Altamonte DriveBrandon: 805 Brandon Town Center DriveFort Myers: 14701 S Tamiami TrailKissimmee: 8160 Irlo Bronson Memorial Hwy.Lutz: 25830 Sierra Center Blvd.Orlando:5620 W. Oak Ridge Road8849 International Drive8735 Vineland Ave.1200 N Alafaya DriveTampa: 3045 N Rocky Point Drive EastHow many Bahama Breeze locations are in Florida?According to the Bahama Breeze location finder on the restaurant's website, there are currently 15 locations in Florida. All of them will either be closed by April 5 or converted. There are 16 locations if you include the Bahama Breeze in the Orlando International Airport (MCO).Florida has the most Bahama Breeze locations of any state, but the restaurant also has locations in Delaware, Georgia, Michigan, New Jersey, North Carolina, Pennsylvania, South Carolina, Virginia, and Washington, according to its website.",
